<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gauja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Livonian Aa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gauja is a river in Latvia, about 450 kilometres long, flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a>. Its lower valley, with towns such as Sigulda and Turaida, was the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> and was conquered by German crusaders in the early 13th century. Much of its valley is protected as Gauja National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gauja.jpg" alt="Gauja" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gatis Pāvils · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ergeljuklintis424aug037qg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gauja flows through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> region of Latvia. It is the only large river of Latvia that begins and ends its flow in the country. About 93.5 kilometres of the Gauja lie within Gauja National Park. There the river flows through the Gauja Valley, which is between 1 and 2.5 kilometres wide and reaches a maximum depth of 85 metres near Sigulda. The sandstone rocks on the banks of the Gauja and of the rivers that join it began to form 370 to 300 million years ago, during the Devonian period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the 13th century the Gauja served as a trade route and as a border river between the Livonian and Latgalian lands. While Livonian languages were still spoken along the river and the sea, it was called Koivo, the Birch River, from the Livonian keùv or the Estonian kõiv. In Latvian the name once meant a great amount or a crowd, and so the river was called the big river. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> suffered greatly during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and from a plague in the 18th century, when the remaining Livonians assimilated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Gauja changes its bed rapidly and has a reputation for being deceitful. Its bed is made of unconsolidated sand and gravel that move with the current. In places the bed is pebbly and forms boulder rapids, among them Kazu, Raiskuma, Rakšu and Ķūķu. Within the national park the river bottom is 60 to 120 metres wide and the depth changes quickly, from 0.3 to 7 metres. In low water the current runs at 0.2 to 0.4 metres a second, and in the spring flood at 2 to 3 metres a second.</p><p>The river usually freezes over in mid-December, and the ice starts moving in late March, though in warm winters it does not freeze. Much underground water flows into it, so its water is cooler than that of the other large rivers of Latvia, and the river changes level and current speed a good deal through the year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gauja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The residents of Riga, allied with the Grand Duchy of Lithuania under the command of Vytenis, decisively defeated the Livonian Order on the banks of the <b>Gauja</b> River near Turaida Castle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-1">1 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The archbishops were princes of the Holy Roman Empire, ruling lands along the Daugava and <b>Gauja</b> through vassals and castles such as Turaida and Kokenhusen.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a></li><li><blockquote>Idumea lay north of the <b>Gauja</b> river, between the Liv lands of Turaida and Metsepole and the Latgalian land of Tālava.</blockquote><a href="/state/idumea">Idumea</a></li><li><blockquote>Metsepole, whose name means 'forest land', was a district of the Finnic-speaking Livonians along the Gulf of Riga north of the <b>Gauja</b>, around modern Limbaži.</blockquote><a href="/state/metsepole">Metsepole</a></li><li><blockquote>Turaida, Thoreida in Latin, was a land of the Livonians, a Finnic people, on the lower course of the <b>Gauja</b> river in modern Latvia.</blockquote><a href="/state/turaida">Turaida</a></li></ul></section>
